    },
    {
      "step": 14,
      "user_message": "What adventurous activity did we decide on for my family vacation? Can you help me recall?",
      "expected_behavior": "Agent should recall from memory: \"Adventure activity\"",
      "agent_response": "I don't have a specific activity recorded — your saved memories show you're planning a family vacation and that you wanted to include an adventure activity, but they don't say which one you chose.\n\nIf you'd like, I can help you recall or pick one. A few quick questions that will narrow it down:\n- Where are you going (or what type of destination — beach, mountains, city)?\n- Ages and activity/fitness levels of family members?\n- Any budget or risk preferences (e.g., gentle vs. high-adrenaline)?\n- How long will you be there?\n\nIf you want quick ideas, here are family-friendly adventurous options and when they work best:\n- Guided day-hike + picnic — great for most ages, low planning.\n- Zip-lining/treetop course — fun and exciting; suitable for older kids/teens.\n- Kayaking or stand-up paddleboarding — good for lakes/coast; low to moderate exertion.\n- Snorkeling or glass-bottom-boat trip — excellent for beach vacations and younger kids.\n- White-water rafting (gentle/grade 2–3) — higher adrenaline, best for teens/adults.\n- Hot-air balloon ride — scenic and memorable, minimal physical effort.\n- Wildlife safari or guided nature tour — educational and adventurous for all ages.\n- Mountain biking or guided e-bike tour — adventurous with adjustable intensity.\n\nTell me your destination, family details, or any preferences and I’ll suggest the best fits, find specific options, and—if you want—save the chosen activity to your memories so you don’t forget. Which would you like to do next?",
      "duration_s": 13.509,
      "passed": false,
      "success_check": {
        "type": "cross_step_semantic_judge",
        "reference_step": 2,
        "extract_focus": "Specific adventure activity",
        "comparison_query": "The agent's response must correctly state the exact \"Adventure activity\" that was previously suggested in the assistant's response. CRITICAL: If the agent says 'I don't have that saved', 'I can't recall', 'I don't have a record', 'I don't have that stored', or similar phrases indicating it doesn't remember, the response should FAIL even if it then suggests the same information. If the agent correctly states the information without saying it doesn't have it saved, the response should PASS.",
        "compare_focus": "Specific adventure activity"
      }
    },
    {
      "step": 15,
      "user_message": "What did we decide in terms of the cake flavor for my friend's birthday party? Can you help me recall?",
      "expected_behavior": "Agent should recall from memory: Cake Flavor",
      "agent_response": "I don't have a specific cake flavor recorded — your memories show you wanted to use a suggested flavor for the birthday, but they don't say which one.\n\nHere are quick, family-friendly options to jog your memory (with when they work best):\n- Chocolate — classic, crowd-pleaser  \n- Vanilla bean — versatile; pairs well with fruit fillings  \n- Red velvet — rich, pairs with cream-cheese frosting  \n- Lemon — light and refreshing for daytime parties  \n- Carrot cake — spiced and substantial, with cream-cheese frosting  \n- Salted caramel or salted-caramel chocolate — slightly more grown-up  \n- Funfetti — festive and great for younger guests\n\nWant me to pick one based on the party (age/theme/dietary needs)? Tell me any allergies, the party theme, and whether you prefer something classic or more adventurous — I’ll recommend one and can remember your choice.",
      "duration_s": 17.708,
      "passed": false,
      "success_check": {
        "type": "cross_step_semantic_judge",
        "reference_step": 4,
        "extract_focus": "Cake Flavor",
        "comparison_query": "The agent must correctly state the specific Cake Flavor from the previous conversation. If the agent explicitly states it doesn't have the information saved (e.g., 'I don't have that saved', 'I can't recall', 'I don't have that stored'), the response should FAIL even if it then suggests something similar. If the agent correctly states the information without indicating it doesn't have it saved, the response should PASS.",
        "compare_focus": "Cake Flavor"
      }
    },
    {
      "step": 16,
      "user_message": "What was the final decision we made regarding the desk chair for my office space redecoration project? Can you help me recall?",
      "expected_behavior": "Agent should recall from memory: \"desk chair recommendation\"",
      "agent_response": "I don’t have a specific chair choice recorded — your memories show you’re redecorating the office but not which desk chair you settled on.\n\nIf you want to jog your memory or decide now, quick questions will help:\n- Budget (roughly)?  \n- How many hours/day do you sit? Any back/neck issues?  \n- Preferred style (modern/minimal, executive leather, mid-century, sporty/gaming)?  \n- Flooring type (hardwood/carpet) and preferred color/material?\n\nQuick recommendations by use-case to help you decide:\n- Best ergonomics (all-day work): mesh or fully adjustable ergonomic chair (e.g., Steelcase Leap, Herman Miller Aeron or more affordable Autonomous/ErgoChair). Good lumbar support, adjustable seat depth/armrests, breathable mesh.  \n- Executive look (formal, leather): high-back leather or faux-leather chair — comfortable and stylish but check lumbar support if you sit long hours.  \n- Budget-friendly / occasional use: basic ergonomic task chair (adjustable height, tilt lock) — ok for shorter sessions.  \n- Stylish / light-duty (matches redecorating aesthetics): upholstered mid-century or designer task chair — looks great but may lack advanced ergonomics.  \n- If you want a mix of comfort + style: mid-range ergonomic with fabric upholstery (adjustable lumbar + headrest).\n\nTell me your answers to the short questions above and I’ll:\n- Recommend 2–3 exact models that match your budget/style, or  \n- Draft an email to order or ask a supplier questions, or  \n- Save your final choice as a note/reminder (if you want me to create a draft/email to yourself for future reference). Which would you like to do next?",
      "duration_s": 21.879,
      "passed": false,
      "success_check": {
        "type": "cross_step_semantic_judge",
        "reference_step": 6,
        "extract_focus": "Desk chair recommendation",
        "comparison_query": "The agent must correctly state the specific \"desk chair recommendation\" from the previous conversation. If the agent explicitly states it doesn't have the information saved (e.g., 'I don't have that saved', 'I can't recall', 'I don't have that stored'), the response should FAIL even if it then suggests something similar. If the agent correctly states the information without indicating it doesn't have it saved, the response should PASS.",
        "compare_focus": "Desk chair recommendation"
      }
    },
    {
      "step": 17,
      "user_message": "What was the icebreaker game we decided on for my team building event? Can you help me recall?",
      "expected_behavior": "Agent should recall from memory: \"Icebreaker game\"",
      "agent_response": "I don't have a specific icebreaker recorded for your team building event — your memories show you're planning the event but not which game you chose.\n\nIf you want, here are concise, easy-to-run icebreaker options (with when they work best). Pick one and I can give a script, materials list, or add it to your agenda.\n\nQuick options\n- Two Truths and a Lie — low-prep, great for small-to-medium groups; helps people learn fun facts.\n- Human Bingo — good for mingling in-person; gets everyone moving and talking.\n- Speed Networking / Lightning Intros — scalable for larger groups; quick 1–1 rotations to surface connections.\n- Marshmallow Challenge (team build) — 15–30 min, hands-on problem solving and teamwork (in-person).\n- Scavenger Hunt — flexible (in-person or hybrid); energizing and collaborative.\n- Show & Tell (object that represents you) — personal, low-pressure, works well for new or remote teams.\n- Office Trivia or Team Facts Quiz — fun, fosters shared history and culture.\n- Common Ground (find things everyone in the small group has in common) — fast, builds rapport and empathy.\n- Photo or Playlist Icebreaker — remote-friendly; ask people to share a photo/track and why.\n\nTell me:\n- Is the event in-person, virtual, or hybrid?\n- Team size?\n- Time available for the icebreaker (5, 15, 30+ minutes)?\n- Tone: fun/energetic, deep/reflective, or problem-solving?\n\nI’ll recommend the best match and can:\n- Give step-by-step instructions and timing,\n- Draft an agenda or an invite message to attendees,\n- Save your chosen icebreaker as a note so you don’t forget.\n\nWhich direction would you like to go?",
      "duration_s": 11.645,
      "passed": false,
      "success_check": {
        "type": "cross_step_semantic_judge",
        "reference_step": 7,
        "extract_focus": "Icebreaker game",
        "comparison_query": "The agent must correctly state the specific \"Icebreaker game\" from the previous conversation. If the agent explicitly states it doesn't have the information saved (e.g., 'I don't have that saved', 'I can't recall', 'I don't have that stored'), the response should FAIL even if it then suggests something similar. If the agent correctly states the information without indicating it doesn't have it saved, the response should PASS.",
        "compare_focus": "Icebreaker game"
      }
    },
    {
      "step": 18,
      "user_message": "What was the specific bookshelf style we discussed for my mini home library setup? Can you help me recall?",
      "expected_behavior": "Agent should recall from memory: \"Bookshelf style\"",
      "agent_response": "I don’t have a specific bookshelf style recorded for your mini home library — your memories show you’re planning the library but not which style you picked.\n\nIf you want, here are practical bookshelf style options with quick pros/cons to help you remember or choose one:\n\n1. Tall, narrow freestanding (e.g., “Billy”-style)\n- Pros: Space-efficient, inexpensive, adjustable shelves; stacks lots of books vertically.\n- Cons: Plain look unless styled; needs wall anchoring for safety.\n- Works if: You have limited floor space and lots of books.\n\n2. Built-in / wall-to-wall shelving\n- Pros: Maximum storage, polished look, custom-fit to the room.\n- Cons: Higher cost and installation time.\n- Works if: You want a statement library and long-term solution.\n\n3. Ladder/leaning bookshelf\n- Pros: Airy, stylish, easy to move; pairs nicely with string lights draped along the top.\n- Cons: Less shelf depth and weight capacity.\n- Works if: You want a casual, decorative library with accent lighting.\n\n4. Cube/modular units (e.g., Kallax)\n- Pros: Flexible configuration, works with baskets/doors, great for combining books + decor.\n- Cons: Cubes can look boxy; may need reinforcement for tall stacks.\n- Works if: You want mix of display + hidden storage; easy to adapt later.\n\n5. Floating wall shelves\n- Pros: Minimalist, keeps floor clear, highlights selected books and decor.\n- Cons: Limited capacity; installation needs good studs/wall anchors.\n- Works if: You want a clean look or are making a small curated collection.\n\n6. Industrial wood + metal bookcase\n- Pros: Sturdy, stylish, great for oversized/heavy books.\n- Cons: Heavier and often pricier.\n- Works if: You prefer a rustic/industrial aesthetic and need durability.\n\nQuick tips that matter for any choice\n- Depth: aim for 10–12\" (25–30 cm) to fit most books comfortably.\n- Adjustable shelves are very useful for mixed sizes.\n- If you like string lights (you mentioned liking them), ladder or built-in shelves are great places to integrate warm LED strips or inexpensive string lights for ambiance.\n- Consider a few closed cabinets/drawers for clutter, and assign a shelf or two for display items.\n\nWant next steps?\n- Tell me room size, number of books (roughly), budget, and preferred aesthetic and I’ll recommend 2–3 exact models or a layout plan.\n- Or tell me which of the styles above sounds right and I’ll save it for you or draft a shopping list/assembly checklist.\n\nWhich direction would you like to go?",
      "duration_s": 18.405,
      "passed": false,
      "success_check": {
        "type": "cross_step_semantic_judge",
        "reference_step": 10,
        "extract_focus": "Bookshelf style",
        "comparison_query": "The agent's response must correctly state the exact \"Bookshelf style\" that was previously suggested in the assistant's response. CRITICAL: If the agent says 'I don't have that saved', 'I can't recall', 'I don't have a record', 'I don't have that stored', or similar phrases indicating it doesn't remember, the response should FAIL even if it then suggests the same information. If the agent correctly states the information without saying it doesn't have it saved, the response should PASS.",
        "compare_focus": "Bookshelf style"
      }
